{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And He took… to work it and to keep it</b> – The plain sense of the verse would indicate that the Garden needed someone to work it and to guard it, but the truth contradicts this. For why would it need someone to guard it? If from thieves — this is absurd, and what work did the Garden require? And if it needed a worker, then for these five thousand five hundred years, who worked it?" "<b>Truly</b>, you must know that everything that exists is but a model for spirituality. Just as the earth needs work, sowing, and watering to produce food for man, as well as guarding from things that destroy and harm growth, so too the Garden of Eden — which today is solely the soil of souls, for bodies have become physicalized — likewise needs work and guarding according to its spiritual nature. The watering specially designated for it is engagement in Torah, as the verse says (Deuteronomy 32:2): ״יַעֲרֹף כַּמָּטַר לִקְחִי תִּזַּל כַּטַּל אִמְרָתִי״, and the seeds are positive commandments that a person performs, as hinted in the prophet's words (Hosea 10:12): ״זִרְעוּ לָכֶם״ and so forth. And even though the prophet mentions only righteousness, he does not speak of the particular case of righteousness alone but of all particular forms of righteousness. And so it is brought in the words of the Sages, may their memory be for blessing (Midrash Tehillim 114), that every commandment a person performs, the Holy One, blessed be He, plants it, etc. And the work is great engagement and intensive contemplation in Torah, which is the essence of its commandment. And the guarding too, so as not to breach its fences and not destroy its plants — and this is the observance of negative commandments, which a person must keep so as not to transgress them, for they destroy every good thing. As they said, may their memory be for blessing (Sotah 21a): ״עֲבֵרָה מְכַבָּה מִצְוָה״ — and even though they said that transgression does not extinguish Torah, their intention is not to say that an evil act does not harm Torah at all, but rather to say that a single transgression has no power to extinguish the Torah. Yet ultimately, when one multiplies destruction, his Torah is corrupted and its lamp dims and its cord turns upon its face — may the Merciful One spare us:" "<b>And now</b> it is justified to say of the Garden of Eden ״to work it and to keep it.״ And behold, in those days when HaShem placed man in the Garden of Eden, man would look and gaze upon the operations of a righteous person for eternal life through his deeds — what he was effecting in the Garden of Eden. But after man was banished, the vision of this matter was taken from him. Nevertheless, the deed itself — the soul labors on his behalf, for the spirituality is not prevented by the distance, and the action is accomplished. Happy is the one who waits and exerts himself in this world, to eat of the fruit of his path, which is the nourishment of the soul in the Garden of Eden. Furthermore, a teaching of the Sages, may their memory be for blessing, came to my hand, and this is their language: ״לְעָבְדָהּ״ — these are positive commandments; ״וּלְשָׁמְרָהּ״ — these are negative commandments. And their intention, may their memory be for blessing, is along the path we have explained."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:38:39.846085959Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}